{
  "dom_id": "step-q50433",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268777/assets/45cfcf09b34366e9d63f61d9be13c0a5804151f5ad1e4411248002d06612281a.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=-\\frac{4}{y}$ and $x=y+5$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{y} & =y+5 \\\\ -4 & ={y}^{2}+5y \\\\ {y}^{2}+5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-4$ to $y=-1.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268777/assets/e559c357286114e2510a5e57016657ade81eb48439fe721f07c0e0380ae5e9d5.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=-\\frac{4}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=y+5.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=y+5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268777/assets/fe0b5d26fd0ccbdb03646aa497226745db8f396e6e0be264691e329a118550e8.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{[\\,(y+5)-(-\\frac{1}{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{[y+\\frac{11}{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=-\\frac{4}{y}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268777/assets/0707f752a19e0dfcbe8defdaa600eeddd02ae98b8c9b35fee8fbf0480dd94be3.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{[\\,(-\\frac{4}{y})-(-\\frac{1}{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4}{y}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{[y+\\frac{11}{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4}{y}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({[y+\\frac{11}{2}]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4}{y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
